// Stages 3 and 4 toward a Radeon that takes commands (a Polaris 10, the
// RX 580), on top of stages 0 to 2 (gpu_engines.mlx): the SMC's own
// firmware uploaded and started, the engines' firmware loaded by the SMC,
// and the two copy engines (SDMA) running their rings, each proved by the
// processor through the GART. Each step is the study's
// (/tmp/brix-next/gpu-accel-kernel.verified.md 3.3, 3.4, 6.2 to 6.6, 8.4,
// 8.7 as its second reading corrected them), built from Linux 6.6's
// amdgpu, with its self-test, its one line in the log and its way back.
//
// THE STEPS (../engine_choice.mlx: the digit 3 goes to F2, 4 to D2):
// 3  F1  the SMC's firmware: the file the card's key names
//        (polaris10_k_smc.bin on the user's card), uploaded into the SMC's
//        RAM and started in protected mode as polaris10_start_smu does
//        (or without protection where the card says so); proved by the
//        SMC's clock, its program counter running in RAM, its firmware's
//        flag and the version word in its RAM, which must be the uploaded
//        payload's own word 5 (not the file header's version: the two
//        differ in polaris10_k_smc.bin). An SMC found running is not
//        uploaded to and not sent the start's trigger. Way back: the SMC
//        held in reset.
//    F2  the engines' firmware: the seven payloads copied into the
//        system's memory the GART maps, the list of them (nine entries) in
//        the card's memory, five messages and LoadUcodes; proved by the
//        load status word (one bit an engine) and no fault. The SMC
//        unhalts the RLC and the compute processor as it loads them: they
//        are halted again as Linux's own stop leaves them, the copy engines
//        too unless stage 4 follows; stage 5 (gpu_compute.mlx) starts them
//        from there as Linux starts a stopped block.
// 4  D1  copy engine 0 started as sdma_v3_0_start starts it, its write
//        pointer by register (no doorbell); Linux's ring test: the engine
//        writes 0xdeadbeef through the GART. Way back: the engine halted,
//        its words as before.
//    D2  both copy engines started anew with their doorbells; Linux's
//        ring test and indirect buffer test on each, word for word; a fill
//        of the card's memory, a copy from the system's memory to the
//        card's and back, each in an indirect buffer framed as Linux frames
//        the test's, each with its fence and trap; proved by the processor
//        (the words through the GART and the aperture, the fences) and by
//        the interrupt ring's entries. Way back: both engines halted, the
//        doorbells off, their words as before.
// The way down (`stop`, the study's 8.7): the copy engines halted, the
// interrupt ring off, the doorbells off; the GART stays on.
//
// AROUND EVERY STEP, as stages 0 to 2 do (gpu_engines.mlx's head): the
// card's temperature read before and after and said, and a step not begun
// at the study's mark (85 C) or above; the picture watched (frames, the
// memory interface, a sum over its memory) with the log's lines kept off
// the screen meanwhile; the card alive afterwards; every register a step
// writes kept first (`put`), put back last first by a step that fails.
// Nothing of power management: no DPM, no clock, voltage or fan message;
// the default levels Linux writes into the SMC on a card with the AVFS
// fuse are not written (the study's 3.3, kept out). No register of the
// display's is written by any step.
//
// The registers and their fields are ../registers' modules, generated from
// Linux 6.6's headers; the other numbers are Linux 6.6's (smu7_ppsmc.h,
// smu74.h, smu_ucode_xfer_vi.h, tonga_sdma_pkt_open.h, amdgpu_doorbell.h;
// smu7_smumgr.c, polaris10_smumgr.c, amdgpu_ucode.c, amdgpu_cgs.c, sdma_v3_0.c,
// amdgpu_ring.c, amdgpu_ib.c, vi.c, gfx_v8_0.c), held by check_gpu_registers.py.
// The card: gpu_engines.mlx's `Hands` and `More` (below); tests/500 and 501's.
const engines = @import("./gpu_engines.mlx")
